window.__hvBdRegister("simplicius-enchiridion", {"key":"simplicius-enchiridion","sections":[{"i":0,"j":16,"loc":"Preface","title":"Purpose of the Manual and the soul as man","summary":"After Stanhope's note on the translation, Simplicius explains that Arrian compiled the Enchiridion from Epictetus's discourses, naming it a manual to be kept at hand like a soldier's sword. Its aim is to set the soul free from fears and passions, and it makes its followers happy in this life whether or not the soul survives death. The precepts are meant not for the perfected or contemplative sage but for those living by reason who use the body as an instrument. Simplicius then sets out Socrates' argument from the Alcibiades that the rational soul alone is the man, since the user of a tool differs from the tool and the body cannot govern itself.","ideas":["The Enchiridion is a practical manual for amending life","Its audience is the person of moral and political virtue, not the contemplative sage","Man is the rational soul using the body as its instrument","Care for body or wealth is care for the instrument, not the self"],"terms":[{"t":"Enchiridion (Manual)","d":"A handbook to be kept always at hand, a name Simplicius links to the soldier's short sword."},{"t":"rational soul","d":"The essence of the human being, which employs the body as a craftsman employs his tools."}]},{"i":17,"j":36,"loc":"Chapter I (1 of 4)","title":"What is in our power, and the Good","summary":"Epictetus divides things into those in our power (opinions, impulses, desires, aversions) and those not. Simplicius explains that these motions arise within the soul even when their objects lie outside it, with opinion first in order. To ground this he traces all things to the Good, which produces first unchanging goods, then self-moving souls in a middle rank, then bodies moved from outside. Human souls join heavenly and earthly natures and so can rise or sink; choosing false pleasure-bound goods causes vice, choosing true good causes virtue. Laws judge actions by intention, which confirms that Epictetus rightly begins with this distinction, limited to things that concern us.","ideas":["The soul's motions are its own, though objects may come from outside","A hierarchy runs from the Good through souls to passive bodies","Pleasure is always mixed with prior pain","Moral worth lies in choice and intention, not the bare act"],"terms":[{"t":"eph' hēmin (in our power)","d":"What depends solely on our own judgment and choice, such as opinion, desire and aversion."},{"t":"aretē (virtue)","d":"Simplicius connects the word with what is chosen, since virtue is both the object and the effect of choice."}]},{"i":37,"j":53,"loc":"Chapter I (2 of 4)","title":"Objections to free will answered","summary":"Simplicius takes up those who deny that anything is in our power, arguing from bodily want, from the compelling force of evident objects, from fixed character and habit, and from a stone's necessary fall. He replies that want produces desire only in beings able to desire, and that the rational soul, standing in a middle rank, can override bodily wants, as in fasting. Objects invite but do not force desire, since the same object moves some and not others, and we can examine whether an apparent good is real. He separates external necessity, which destroys freedom, from the internal necessity of acting by one's own nature. Souls change between vice and virtue by their own choice, so God is not to blame for wickedness.","ideas":["Want alone does not create desire","The rational soul has a threefold disposition: toward body, itself, and what is above","Objects propose themselves; they do not drag the soul","Inner necessity of one's nature is compatible with freedom"],"terms":[{"t":"twofold necessity","d":"External compulsion, which abolishes choice, versus the inner necessity of acting according to one's own self-moving nature."}]},{"i":54,"j":58,"loc":"Chapter I (3 of 4)","title":"Astrology and fate do not compel choice","summary":"Simplicius answers the claim that the heavens' revolution fixes our desires. If the rational soul is immortal, it does not depend on matter and motion; only its bodily instrument does, and astrologers infer the soul's temper from that instrument, as one judges a craftsman by his tools. Souls are brought under positions of the stars that suit their deserts, much as festivals gather like-minded people. Souls choose their way of life by their former disposition, but how they live within it is their own. Stellar influences can be received moderately or excessively, and vice comes from the corrupted mind, not the stars, just as blindness from gazing at the sun is the gazer's folly.","ideas":["Astrology reads the body, the soul's instrument, not the soul itself","Celestial positions match souls by likeness, without compelling them","We are blamed or praised for conduct within a calling, not the calling","Evil arises from lack or misuse of power, not from excess of good"],"terms":[{"t":"heimarmenē (fate)","d":"The ordered revolution of the heavens, which Simplicius says shapes bodies but cannot bind the rational soul's choice."}]},{"i":59,"j":71,"loc":"Chapter I (4 of 4)","title":"Mixed actions; things outside our power","summary":"Simplicius gives general replies: denying freedom destroys the soul's self-motion, ignores our felt power to assent and refuse, and abolishes praise, blame, law and social order. Even under tyranny or passion we choose the lesser evil, so compulsion still leaves choice, though only being pushed bodily is truly passive. He distinguishes the voluntary, chosen for its own sake, from the free, and calls actions partly willed and partly unwilled mixed. He then explains why body, property, reputation and office lie outside our power: the soul contributes to them but depends on many other causes. The body is listed first because its needs lead to all the rest.","ideas":["Self-motion is the essence of soul","Denying freedom abolishes morality and law","Voluntary versus free; mixed actions","Body, wealth, fame and office need external helps"],"terms":[{"t":"mixed actions","d":"Acts chosen under pressure that are partly voluntary and partly involuntary, as in Homer's divided heart."}]},{"i":72,"j":84,"loc":"Chapters II–III","title":"Free things versus servile things","summary":"Epictetus says what is in our power is free and unhindered, and what is not is weak, servile and another's. Simplicius explains each quality: our own acts are self-sufficient and cannot be overruled, while external things depend on others and are not truly ours. Chapter III warns that mistaking the servile for the free brings hindrance, grief and blame of gods and men. Simplicius adds that external goods both disappoint and distract us from our real good, while one who rests happiness in regulated desires and aversions can have no enemy and fear no one. Citing Plato, he argues the virtuous life is also the most pleasant, since reformed debauchees turn temperate but not the reverse.","ideas":["Our own acts are free, firm and properly ours","External things are servile and belong to others","Misplaced desire breeds grief and blame of God","The virtuous life is the pleasantest as well as the best"],"terms":[]},{"i":85,"j":101,"loc":"Chapters IV–V","title":"Whole-hearted pursuit; testing appearances","summary":"Epictetus urges that the pursuit of freedom be total: some things must be given up entirely and others postponed, or one loses both. Simplicius explains that vicious goods must be renounced, while innocent ones like marriage and household are suspended until beginners are fortified; vice comes from ignorance or unruly passion, as with Medea. A divided life is less vicious than a worldly one but more miserable. He notes Epictetus's repeated word remember, addressed to a soul darkened by matter. Chapter V teaches meeting a frightening impression as only an appearance and testing it, above all by whether it concerns things in our power.","ideas":["Renounce vicious goods; postpone innocent ones","Vice stems from ignorance or ungoverned passion","A divided life is a perpetual self-contradiction","Test every impression by what is in our power"],"terms":[{"t":"phantasia (impression)","d":"The appearance a thing makes in the mind, which may represent it truly or deceptively and must be examined."}]},{"i":102,"j":119,"loc":"Chapters VI–VII","title":"Aversion now, desire postponed","summary":"Epictetus says desire aims at getting, aversion at avoiding, and that aversions limited to what is in our power cannot fail; he advises suspending desire for now and using only gentle impulse. Simplicius notes Epictetus argues like Socrates from the common notion that happiness is getting what one wants. He then addresses the difficulty of forbidding even good desires: Epictetus speaks to beginners, or means only to curb vehemence. Change must be gradual, as with a glutton cutting meals; aversion is allowed first because, as Hippocrates says of the body, corruption must be purged before nourishment helps. Beginners are not yet ready for desires beyond their strength.","ideas":["Aversions confined to our power never miss","Epictetus starts from common notions of happiness","Reform must be gradual, not violent","Purging vice must precede nourishment in virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"hormē (impulse)","d":"The soul's first motion toward or away from an object, which beginners should use gently and cautiously."}]},{"i":120,"j":131,"loc":"Chapter VIII","title":"Remembering the nature of loved things","summary":"Epictetus advises reflecting on what each cherished thing is, starting with an earthen cup and rising to wife and child as mortals. Simplicius classes external things as those that delight, those that are useful, and those we love by natural kinship, listing examples of each. Meditating on their perishability is a rehearsal of their loss that prevents shock. Following the proverb that the potter tries a cup before a jar, one must start with small things and advance gradually. By this method we deal with what is not in our power as if it were, since composure at a child's death is ours.","ideas":["Three kinds of attachment: pleasure, use, natural affection","Meditating on a thing's nature rehearses its loss","Train on trifles before great losses","Mere amusements deserve no consideration from the proficient"],"terms":[]},{"i":132,"j":140,"loc":"Chapter IX","title":"Foreseeing what attends each action","summary":"Epictetus tells us to picture beforehand what usually happens at the bath, so that if hindered we recall that our aim was also to keep mind and reason undisturbed. Simplicius advises weighing each action's likely accidents, avoiding needless risks as Cato regretted a sea voyage, and preparing for unavoidable ones. He answers objections that foresight breeds cowardice or gloom and that Demosthenes' advice to hope well is better. Hope of sure success makes reverses unbearable, while the soul's good from virtuous action outweighs bodily or financial loss, as Menoeceus showed. The trivial example trains us for greater trials.","ideas":["Forethought prevents surprise and needless danger","Hoping for certain success makes failure harder to bear","The soul's gain from virtue outweighs external loss","Small examples prepare for weighty affairs"],"terms":[]},{"i":141,"j":153,"loc":"Chapter X (1 of 2)","title":"Opinions, not things, disturb us","summary":"Epictetus says men are troubled by their opinions about things, not things themselves; death is not dreadful, or Socrates would have thought it so. Simplicius sets out the argument: what is evil by nature appears so to all, yet death did not appear so to Socrates, so the fear comes from opinion, like honey tasting bitter in jaundice. He cites Plato's Phaedo and Laws that death is better than embodied life. The argument extends to pain, which people accept for cures and Spartan youths bore for glory, and to poverty, which Crates embraced. Right opinion lets us manage what is not in our power and even profit from afflictions.","ideas":["Death is not evil by nature, as Socrates' calm shows","False opinion is like a jaundiced palate","Pain and poverty are endurable and sometimes chosen","Blame for disturbance lies with our own opinions"],"terms":[{"t":"dogma (opinion)","d":"A judgment about things, which Epictetus says is the real source of distress."}]},{"i":154,"j":163,"loc":"Chapter X (2 of 2)","title":"The uneducated, the beginner, the philosopher","summary":"Epictetus says the uneducated blame others, the beginner blames himself, and the educated blames neither. Simplicius explains that the perfect philosopher never meets evil, since reason rules and passion obeys. The beginner knows the distinction between what is and is not in our power but still errs because passions resist reason, as with Medea, so he rightly blames himself. The ignorant err through both passion and ignorance, taking body or wealth for themselves, and blame others like boys who hate their masters. Simplicius closes with the image of the sensual part as a child and reason as its tutor.","ideas":["Three grades of moral progress","Knowing good precedes mastering the passions","The ignorant locate good and evil outside themselves","Reason is tutor to the childlike sensual part"],"terms":[{"t":"prokoptōn (one making progress)","d":"The beginner in philosophy who still errs but rightly locates the fault in himself."}]},{"i":164,"j":172,"loc":"Chapter XI","title":"Pride only in one's own excellence","summary":"Epictetus forbids being elated at an excellence not one's own, such as a horse's beauty; one's own is the right use of impressions. Simplicius takes elation to mean thinking oneself better through some added good, and argues every good belongs to its own subject: an axe's sharpness does not make a carpenter. He identifies the right use of impressions with true opinion, then with right desire and aversion, and finally with conduct matching one's judgments. The incontinent desire virtue weakly but are swept off by passion, as with Medea. A philosopher's excellence, like a carpenter's, lies in acting by right rule.","ideas":["A possession's excellence is not its owner's","Right use of impressions means true judgment","It also means right desire and matching conduct","Weak right desire is overborne in the incontinent"],"terms":[{"t":"chrēsis phantasiōn (use of impressions)","d":"Managing appearances so that judgment, desire and action accord with the truth of things."}]},{"i":173,"j":178,"loc":"Chapter XII","title":"The ship's captain and the call","summary":"Epictetus compares life to a voyage: ashore for water one may gather shell-fish or salads, but must watch for the captain's call and leave all, lest one be bound like sheep; the old should stay near the ship. Simplicius says the passage permits marriage and other goods with limits: necessities first, conveniences like wife and estate as incidental extras, and pleasures, riches and honors excluded. He interprets the allegory: the sea is mortal life, the ship what binds soul to body, the captain God, the port one's birth station, the water necessities, and the call death. Those who cling are torn away unwillingly; the old should stay unencumbered.","ideas":["Necessities, incidental goods and excluded goods ranked","Marriage allowed to those who have made progress","The voyage allegory of soul, body and God","Readiness to obey the call to die"],"terms":[]},{"i":179,"j":192,"loc":"Chapter XIII (1 of 4)","title":"Wishing things as they are; bodies","summary":"Epictetus advises wanting events to happen as they do. Simplicius states the objection that no decent person could be pleased by earthquakes, plagues, wars, cruelty and the loss of arts. He answers that if these were truly evil neither good men could welcome them nor could God be loved, but they are not. Bodies and irrational souls follow higher causes like shadows and have no merit of their own. Changes in bodies are no evil: dissolution returns elements to their own masses, and the corruption of parts serves the whole, as nature and surgeons sacrifice parts to save a body.","ideas":["The problem of natural and human calamities","Bodies obey higher causes and cannot be harmed by change","Corruption of one thing generates another","Parts are sacrificed for the good of the whole"],"terms":[]},{"i":193,"j":206,"loc":"Chapter XIII (2 of 4)","title":"Afflictions as medicine and exercise","summary":"Simplicius turns to souls. Irrational souls follow their bodies, beasts showing a trace of inner impulse; the rational soul uses the body freely and, if it keeps its rank, is not harmed by bodily pain, as Socrates said the pain was in his leg. A soul that clings to the body needs bitter remedies, as nurses put wormwood on the breast to wean infants. Fear of pain is a first step toward choosing virtue freely. Afflictions also exercise the healthy, as with Hercules, Theseus, Diogenes and Socrates. Since God acts as physician, friend and father, calamities are good or at least necessary means, and the wise man is content.","ideas":["Misfortune weans the soul from bodily pleasure","Fear of pain leads gradually to free choice of virtue","Hardship trains and displays the virtuous","God acts like a physician, not a tormentor"],"terms":[{"t":"necessary means","d":"Things chosen not for themselves but because a true good cannot be had without them, like surgery for health."}]},{"i":207,"j":218,"loc":"Chapter XIII (3 of 4)","title":"Vice, free choice, and God's innocence","summary":"Simplicius asks whether the disease itself, especially the soul's vice, makes God a cause of evil. Bodily disease harms neither body nor soul and serves the cycle of the universe. Vice arises from our own desires and aversions, so it is ours, not God's. Human virtues require a soul capable of corruption, as health requires a body capable of sickness; a middle rank of beings had to exist, and vice has only an accidental being. The soul never chooses evil as evil, and the power of free choice is itself a great good. Simplicius notes that Epictetus means contentment with bodily and external events, not with vice.","ideas":["Vice is the soul's own doing, not God's","Human virtue presupposes the possibility of corruption","Evil has only an added, accidental existence","Freedom of choice is a royal privilege"],"terms":[{"t":"accidental being","d":"Simplicius's notion that evil has no positive existence of its own but only a dependent, added one."}]},{"i":219,"j":223,"loc":"Chapter XIII (4 of 4)","title":"Sickness hinders the body, not the will","summary":"Epictetus says sickness hinders the body but not choice, and lameness the leg but not the will. Simplicius sets out the argument: even if misfortunes were ours they would deserve patience as beneficial, but they belong to body, foot or estate, not to the rational soul, so being upset turns a foreign ill into our own. He also answers the objection that sickness and poverty make right living impossible, citing Diogenes, Crates and Zeno. He ends with Epictetus himself, a lame, sickly slave whose cottage needed no lock, who spoke from experience.","ideas":["Hindrance to the body is not hindrance to the self","Disturbance is our own true evil","Poverty did not stop Diogenes, Crates or Zeno","Epictetus lived what he taught"],"terms":[{"t":"prohairesis (choice, will)","d":"The free principle in which human being consists, which no sickness or poverty can confine."}]},{"i":224,"j":241,"loc":"Chapters XIV–XV","title":"Inner powers against accidents; losses as restitution","summary":"Simplicius explains Epictetus' advice to turn inward at every accident and find the matching power: continence against beauty, hardiness against toil, meekness against slander. He argues that God, in sending the soul down into bodily life, equipped it with faculties for every trial, and distinguishes the continence and hardiness of beginners from the temperance and fortitude of the perfect. On Chapter XV he defends the rule to say 'I have restored it' rather than 'I have lost it': wife, child and estate were lent by the Giver, who may reclaim them by any means. Possessions are to be held like a traveller's inn, and training should begin with lesser losses.","ideas":["Providence gives the soul a power suited to each trial it meets","Continence (struggle with passion) differs from temperance (passion fully obedient)","Slander harms only the slanderer, and true slander exposes our faults","Speaking of goods as returned, not lost, reshapes our attachment to them"],"terms":[{"t":"sōphrosynē (temperance)","d":"The state in which the passions obey reason without struggle, which Simplicius says preserves the mind's rule."},{"t":"enkrateia (continence)","d":"The beginner's virtue of reason overcoming passions that still rebel against it."}]},{"i":242,"j":254,"loc":"Chapters XVI–XVII","title":"Anxiety for livelihood and practice with small losses","summary":"Epictetus says it is better to starve with a calm mind than live in plenty with anxiety, and better a servant be vicious than oneself be miserable trying to prevent it. Simplicius answers the objection that this neglect of the world leads to ruin: beginners whose passions still run high are endangered by worldly business, while accomplished philosophers can manage both. He notes Epictetus could have pointed to the philosopher's frugality and the help of friends, but chose the boldest form of the claim. He then defends gradual training on trifles like spilled oil and stolen wine, warns against imitating Crates' sudden renunciation, and recommends forethought, which takes the sting out of both misfortune and sudden good fortune.","ideas":["Tranquillity of mind outweighs material security","A man without virtue cannot make another virtuous","Small losses are the price paid for constancy, a bargain worth making","Premeditation of what may happen keeps the mind steady"],"terms":[{"t":"prokoptōn (proficient)","d":"The learner still making progress in virtue, whom Epictetus is mainly addressing here."}]},{"i":255,"j":270,"loc":"Chapters XVIII–XIX","title":"Indifference to reputation and futile wishes","summary":"Epictetus tells the learner to accept being thought foolish for neglecting the world and not to affect being thought wise. Simplicius explains that fear of seeming odd drives much worldly care, and that the opinion of the many is no standard; reason and the judgment of the few good are. He notes the wording 'affect to be thought', since others' esteem is not in our power but courting it is, and that praise should make the beginner more distrustful of himself. On Chapter XIX he sets out what is worth pursuing: things possible, our own, and lasting. Wishing that loved ones never die or that a vicious servant behave well is wishing for what belongs to another or for the impossible.","ideas":["Being mad for fear of being called a fool is absurd","Pursuing virtue and worldly fame at once is difficult, though not impossible for rare minds","Worthy objects of effort are possible, one's own, and durable","Only desires confined to what is in our power cannot be frustrated"],"terms":[]},{"i":271,"j":287,"loc":"Chapters XX–XXI","title":"Slavery to desire and life as a banquet","summary":"Epictetus says whoever can grant or take away what we desire or fear is our master. Simplicius develops the image: the worldly man has countless masters, serves without rest, is a slave of slaves since his appetite obeys outer objects, and chooses his chains. Chapter XXI compares life to a feast given by God: take what is offered modestly, do not snatch at what has not reached you, and let go what passes on. Those who refuse even what is offered, like Diogenes and Heraclitus, become partners with the gods. Simplicius tells the story of Diogenes asking Alexander to stand out of his sunlight.","ideas":["Liberty requires confining desire and aversion to what is our own","The worldly man's bondage is continuous and self-chosen","Moderate acceptance of goods makes one a worthy guest of the gods","Disdain for offered goods brings a share in divine dignity"],"terms":[]},{"i":288,"j":307,"loc":"Chapters XXII–XXIV","title":"Grieving neighbours, the play of life, omens","summary":"Epictetus warns that a neighbour weeping over loss is troubled by his opinion, not the event; one may outwardly share his grief but not inwardly. Simplicius argues that if the event were evil by nature it would grieve everyone, citing Anaxagoras on his son's death, and that compassion must keep its bounds, like a rescuer who neither stays on the bank nor is swept away. Chapter XXIII compares the world to a theatre: God the poet assigns the part, and our task is to play it well, as Epictetus the lame slave did better than many emperors. Simplicius thinks Chapter XXIV is misplaced and should follow XXII: omens may threaten body, estate or family but never the reasoning self, which can turn any disaster to profit.","ideas":["Grief comes from opinion, which is our own and in our power","Genuine pity is allowed but must not infect the mind","Unlike the feast, the play does not let us refuse our part; only how we act it is ours","What can be turned to good is not truly evil"],"terms":[]},{"i":308,"j":331,"loc":"Chapters XXV–XXVIII","title":"Invincibility, envy, insults, and remembering death","summary":"Simplicius reads Chapter XXV as proof of the preceding one: if we enter only contests within our will we cannot be defeated, and so cannot be made miserable. On Chapter XXVI he argues that since the promoted and honoured gain nothing that is truly human good, there is no ground for envy; he distinguishes envy, always vicious, from emulation, which is praiseworthy when virtue is the prize. After Epictetus' short Chapter XXVII on insults and blows, Simplicius presents objectors who say contempt of the world exposes philosophers to every injury. He answers that the harm lies in opinion, recommends delay and silence on the example of Socrates, and joins XXVIII to the preceding with 'but': keep death and exile always in view.","ideas":["No defeat is possible in contests confined to our own choices","Envy targets equals; emulation of virtue is commendable","True slander should shame us for the deed; false slander harms the speaker","Constant meditation on death removes its terror and curbs desire"],"terms":[{"t":"phthonos (envy)","d":"Distress at another's apparent happiness, which Simplicius calls always a vice."},{"t":"zēlos (emulation)","d":"The striving to equal or surpass others, good when aimed at virtue and bad when aimed at fortune."}]},{"i":332,"j":347,"loc":"Chapters XXIX–XXX","title":"Ridicule of new philosophers and self-approval","summary":"Epictetus warns those turning to philosophy to expect mockery, to avoid haughtiness, and to hold their post as assigned by God. Simplicius describes why old friends and relatives mock or obstruct the convert, and admits that conceited beginners often deserve it, since arrogance contradicts 'know thyself'. He argues the scoffers themselves honour philosophy, will admire perseverance, and that yielding to them earns a double scorn. On Chapter XXX he explains the advice to be content with being, not seeming, good and to seek one's own approval. He reconciles this with the earlier 'do not affect to be thought wise' by the different stage of the person addressed, and offers a second reading as a correction against valuing the world's esteem.","ideas":["Mockery of converts springs from anger, envy and fear of being shown up","Haughtiness is a swelling from disease, not true greatness of soul","Love of honour can serve virtue when it seeks the approval of the wise","The advanced learner may judge himself; the beginner may not"],"terms":[{"t":"gnōthi seauton (know thyself)","d":"The rule Simplicius calls the first principle and highest precept of all philosophy."}]},{"i":348,"j":358,"loc":"Chapter XXXI (first part)","title":"Obscurity is not a real disgrace","summary":"Epictetus' long chapter answers the fear of living without honour, of being useless to friends and to one's country. Simplicius says such objections occur to advanced learners who aim at honour and service, not to beginners worried about starving. He reconstructs Epictetus' argument as a syllogism: disgrace is an evil, evil is in our power, so no one else can disgrace us. He defends each premise, then discusses the difficult sentence comparing evil with baseness, offering two readings, one of them by transposing the negative particle.","ideas":["Public office and invitations are not ours to give ourselves, so their lack is no evil","Dishonour is evil because honour belongs to the best beings","The field of human good is the use of one's own will","Another can no more make us miserable than make us base"],"terms":[{"t":"adoxia (obscurity, disrepute)","d":"Lack of public honour, which Epictetus argues cannot be an evil since it is not in our power."}]},{"i":359,"j":372,"loc":"Chapter XXXI (second part)","title":"Serving friends and country without losing virtue","summary":"Simplicius takes up the objection that a philosopher without wealth or influence cannot help friends. Epictetus answers that no one can give what he lacks, and that friends who ask him to trade fidelity and modesty for money act unjustly and foolishly; a faithful friend is worth more than a gainful one. Against the claim that he does his country no service, Epictetus says each serves in his own trade, and making a good citizen is a great service. Simplicius adds that a well-ordered state would honour the philosopher as a maker of men and choose him as leader, citing Socrates at Delium and Xenophon's retreat. Corrupt states stifle virtue, yet a rare virtuous soul shines brighter in them.","ideas":["Wealth and office may be pursued only if virtue is kept intact","Friendship is equality; demanding a friend's ruin violates it","The philosopher serves the state by forming good men","Adversity in a bad state can perfect exceptional virtue"],"terms":[]},{"i":373,"j":382,"loc":"Chapter XXXII","title":"Being passed over: the price of lettuce","summary":"Epictetus addresses the philosopher left out of invitations and consultations: if these honours are good, rejoice for the other; if evil, be glad to miss them; and they are bought with flattery, as lettuce is bought for a coin. Simplicius notes he could have used the general distinction of what is in our power but chose a more specific argument. He adds 'indifferent' to the division, then dwells on the goodwill shown in rejoicing at another's good, arguing that a will that wishes good to all is the soul's likeness to God, whose power and will make all things good. He concludes it is both impossible and unjust to expect the rewards of courtiers without paying their price.","ideas":["Rejoicing at another's good fortune imitates God's universal goodwill","Human virtue lies in willing, since the power to effect is not ours","Honours from the great are sold for flattery and servility","The one who declines the price keeps his freedom as his gain"],"terms":[]},{"i":383,"j":392,"loc":"Chapter XXXIII","title":"Judging our own losses like others'","summary":"Epictetus observes that we call a neighbour's broken cup, or his bereavement, a common event, yet rage when the same happens to us. Simplicius distinguishes common notions all people share, such as that good is profitable and two and two make four, from particular opinions that err through the senses, pleasure, or hasty argument. Our calm judgment of others' misfortunes reflects the common notions; our own passion distorts them. He gives two causes: the soul's excessive sympathy with the mortal body, and refusal to think of death. Since mourners grow calm with time, meditating on separation beforehand should achieve the same, along with moderating our fondness.","ideas":["Universal common notions are reliable; particular opinions often are not","We judge others' losses by nature and our own by passion","Time reconciles mourners; forethought could do so in advance","Excessive fondness for things makes their loss unbearable"],"terms":[{"t":"koinai ennoiai (common notions)","d":"Conceptions shared by all people, such as that the good is beneficial, which Simplicius treats as sure guides to nature."}]},{"i":393,"j":414,"loc":"Chapter XXXIV (first part)","title":"Against two principles: refuting the Manichees","summary":"On Epictetus' line that no one sets up a mark in order to miss it, so nature makes nothing evil, Simplicius opens a long discussion of evil. He first attacks the doctrine of two first principles, good and evil: contraries need a common genus, and many must derive from one. He then describes the dualists' teachings, including God surrendering parts of himself as souls to evil, the five dens of evil, the pillars and twelve windows, eclipses as veils, and the moon gathering souls, and calls them impious and absurd. He argues their scheme makes evil stronger than God and, by compelling the will, abolishes evil itself. Finally he rejects the view that God causes evil, since good cannot produce its contrary.","ideas":["Contraries presuppose a common genus, so they cannot be first principles","Dualism makes God fearful, unjust and maimed","A compelled will commits no sin, so a principle of evil abolishes evil","Perfect goodness cannot be the source of evil"],"terms":[{"t":"archē (principle)","d":"A first source of being; Simplicius argues there can be only one."}]},{"i":415,"j":430,"loc":"Chapter XXXIV (second part)","title":"Evil as privation; bodily corruption","summary":"Simplicius asks what evil is before asking its cause. A substantial evil would have a form and so be good; moral evil is an accident in a subject, a falling-away from nature like limping or missing the mark. All things act for a real or apparent good, and no one chooses evil as evil. When an objector asks what then causes evil, he answers that God made every rank of good, including the lowest, which can change and decay, so that the universe would be complete. The dissolution of bodies frees elements, feeds new births and serves the whole, so it is a necessary, lesser good rather than evil.","ideas":["Evil has no subsistence of its own; it is a parasitic addition to good","Good is prior, as hitting the mark is prior to missing it","Every agent aims at some real or seeming good","Bodily decay serves the whole and is a lesser, necessary good"],"terms":[{"t":"accident (symbebēkos)","d":"What exists only in a subject and can come and go without destroying it, which Simplicius says moral evil is."},{"t":"sterēsis (privation)","d":"A lack or falling short of a natural form, as limping is of walking."}]},{"i":431,"j":451,"loc":"Chapter XXXIV (third part)","title":"Evil of the soul and free will","summary":"Simplicius turns to evil in its strictest sense, the vices of the soul. The human soul stands between higher souls fixed in good and the souls of beasts, and freely sinks into the body, so that reason becomes subject to its slave. Choice is the soul's own act, as laws that judge by intention show, and evil is chosen only under the appearance of good. God gave the power to choose; taking it away would destroy human nature and virtue and leave a gap in creation, and God permits passions to rage as a surgeon lets wounds ripen. Simplicius notes the dualists concede the soul can be depraved, then returns to Epictetus, setting out his saying as a syllogism: evil is a missing of the mark, so nature never designed it.","ideas":["Moral evil arises from the soul's free descent into bodily life","Free will is the greatest good in the lower world","God causes the liberty of choice, not the turning from good","Evil cannot be a purpose of nature, as no archer aims to miss"],"terms":[{"t":"to eph' hēmin (what is in our power)","d":"The sphere of free choice that Simplicius makes the sole source of moral evil."}]},{"i":452,"j":460,"loc":"Chapters XXXIV (second), XXXV–XXXVI","title":"Prostituting the mind; counting the cost","summary":"Epictetus says we would resent anyone handing our body over to abuse, yet we hand our mind to every railer; therefore weigh any undertaking before beginning it. His Olympic athlete example lists the regimen and risks involved, and the man who copies every profession like children at play. Chapter XXXVI applies this to philosophy: it costs sleep, labour, contempt and loss of preferment, and one must choose between caring for oneself or for the world. Simplicius comments that injuring oneself is worse than being injured by another and the soul matters more than the body. He notes why Epictetus says shame rather than anger, since shame belongs to our own voluntary faults.","ideas":["Letting slander disturb us is self-inflicted harm to the mind","Every undertaking must be judged by its full cost","Philosophy's price is high, but it buys tranquillity and freedom","Anger fits wrongs from others; shame fits wrongs we do ourselves"],"terms":[]},{"i":461,"j":477,"loc":"Chapter XXXVII (first part)","title":"Duties from relations: parents, brothers, teachers","summary":"Epictetus says duties are measured by relations: a harsh father is still a father, and a brother's wrong does not release us. Simplicius treats duty as justice broadly, notes that Epictetus compresses what Nicolaus of Damascus discussed at length, and divides duty into duty to equals, superiors, inferiors and oneself. He classifies relations as natural or chosen, between equals or unequals, implying nearness or distance. Chosen bonds like friendship can be dissolved, but natural ones cannot, so a vicious father is owed obedience except where the soul would be harmed. He adds the Roman paternal power, the duty to win over a hostile brother, and the even greater debt owed to teachers who form our souls.","ideas":["Duty follows the relation, not the other person's conduct","Relations by nature are permanent; relations by choice can be ended","Obedience to parents is limited only by duty to God","Teachers deserve honour as shapers of the soul"],"terms":[{"t":"kathēkon (appropriate duty)","d":"What is fitting to do toward another, determined by one's relation to him."}]},{"i":478,"j":493,"loc":"Chapter XXXVII (second part)","title":"Digression on choosing and keeping friends","summary":"Simplicius digresses on friendship, first on choosing friends: look for like temper, past conduct toward friends, and above all whether a man is ruled by reason or passion. Those who prize worldly goods destroy friendship's equality, while goods of the soul grow by being shared. He gives rules of conduct: treat friends as we would be treated, rate their favours above ours, and correct gently, citing a Pythagorean verse. He lists friendship's benefits, including two souls and bodies, Alexander's reply that his friends were his treasure, and friendship as a school of virtue. Because friendship is chosen it binds more strongly than nature, and as the Pythagoreans held, it images union with God; he ends by apologising for his length.","ideas":["Friends should be chosen by character and governance by reason","Goods of the soul increase when shared","A friend is a second self","Friendship requires all the virtues and mirrors union with God"],"terms":[{"t":"philia (friendship)","d":"The chosen bond of equals, which the Pythagoreans placed above all other virtues."}]},{"i":494,"j":502,"loc":"Chapter XXXVII (third part)","title":"Citizens, neighbours, strangers, soldiers, magistrates","summary":"Simplicius returns to the relations Epictetus names. Fellow citizens are a kind of kin, children of a common parent, owed help each in his own way, and neighbours are still nearer, placed by providence. Strangers are under God's special protection: they must never be wronged and should be aided and sped home. A soldier owes swift, brave obedience and even his life to his commander, as Xenophon showed with the death of Cyrus. Good magistrates deserve obedience and help in their burdens, while bad ones are still owed the respect due to their office, so far as conscience allows. Simplicius ends by turning back to his task of explaining the following chapters.","ideas":["Country stands to its citizens as parents to children","Hospitality to strangers is a loan to God","A general's death can undo a victory","Even unworthy rulers are owed respect for their office within conscience"],"terms":[]},{"i":503,"j":517,"loc":"Chapter XXXVIII (1 of 5)","title":"Right notions of the gods and worship","summary":"Epictetus' chapter makes the first duty of religion holding just notions of the gods: that they exist and govern all things justly and well, so that one submits to events willingly. Simplicius explains that such piety is impossible while one places good and evil in externals, since everyone blames the cause of his disappointments, as sons blame fathers, farmers the weather and merchants the market. He then turns to sacrifice: worship purifies soul and body, returns a part to the giver of the whole, and benefits us rather than God. Offerings should follow the custom of one's country, be made with care and purity, and be moderate, neither grudging nor profuse.","ideas":["Piety rests on three beliefs: the gods exist, they govern, and they govern justly","Whoever places happiness in externals will inevitably accuse providence","Offerings serve the worshipper, not God, who needs nothing","Worship should follow ancestral custom and be neither sordid nor extravagant"],"terms":[{"t":"eusebeia (piety)","d":"Reverence for the gods grounded in correct beliefs about them and in contented acceptance of what they send."},{"t":"first fruits","d":"The portion of one's goods returned to God as an acknowledgment of his gifts, not as a bribe."}]},{"i":518,"j":529,"loc":"Chapter XXXVIII (2 of 5)","title":"Proving God exists: causes and motion","summary":"Simplicius undertakes to prove the three points behind the chapter, starting with the existence of a divine first cause. He notes the near-universal agreement of mankind and explains that doubt usually comes from the prosperity of the wicked. Deriving theos from a word for running, he argues that orderly natural and chosen productions have definite causes, which must be traced back to uncaused, eternal ones. Motion likewise requires a first self-moving principle, since an infinite chain of movers is impossible and nothing at rest starts motion. First principles must be simple, indivisible and self-moving, and bodies, being composite of matter and form, cannot be first principles.","ideas":["Almost all peoples agree there is a God; Theophrastus' Acrotheites are the exception","Ordered production points to determinate causes, ending in eternal ones","Mechanical motion must start from a self-moving source","Body, being composite and divisible, cannot be a first principle"],"terms":[{"t":"self-moving (autokinētos)","d":"What moves itself wholly from within, needed as the source of all motion passed from one thing to another."},{"t":"first principle (archē)","d":"An uncaused, simple and eternal cause from which other things derive their being and motion."}]},{"i":530,"j":540,"loc":"Chapter XXXVIII (3 of 5)","title":"From Soul to Intellect to the One","summary":"The self-mover is identified as soul, which moves bodies from within and holds the pure forms that it gives to them; there are celestial and sublunary souls. Because soul still changes in its acts of thinking and judging, it must depend on something wholly unmoved, as the steady cycles of elements, seasons and species also show. Ascending further, Simplicius locates the primary forms, united yet distinct, in an eternal unchanging divine intellect. Above many principles stands the One before the many, the cause of causes and God of gods, who is the supreme good, power and knowledge. This first cause has no proper name; we can only gather the best attributes of creatures and apply them to him.","ideas":["Soul is the self-moving source of bodily motion and form","The changeable self-mover presupposes an unmoved, unchanging cause","Forms exist in the divine intellect, united yet unconfused","The One before the many is the first cause and cannot be properly named"],"terms":[{"t":"the One before the many","d":"The single cause that precedes and contains all plural principles, as opposed to a one that is a member of a group."},{"t":"forms (eidē)","d":"Original patterns of things that exist purely in soul and intellect and only imperfectly in bodies."}]},{"i":541,"j":556,"loc":"Chapter XXXVIII (4 of 5)","title":"Defending providence against the deniers","summary":"Simplicius addresses those who grant God but deny that he cares for human affairs, chiefly because the wicked prosper and the good suffer. He sets out a dilemma: neglect would have to come from ignorance, lack of power or lack of will, and each option is absurd for a creator who is wise, mighty and good. Human affairs are not too small for him, and care of the whole requires care of the parts, as with a physician or general. God governs without effort through fixed laws, guardian spirits and an ever-present goodness like the sun's light. The supposed prosperity of the wicked is not true happiness, and external goods and evils are used to cure, test or punish souls.","ideas":["Neglect by God would imply ignorance, weakness or unwillingness, all absurd","Providence over the whole must include the parts","God governs effortlessly, like the sun shining on all things","The good cannot truly be wretched, nor the wicked truly happy"],"terms":[{"t":"pronoia (providence)","d":"God's continual care and government of the world down to its smallest parts."},{"t":"guardian spirits (daimones)","d":"Beings God sets over humans to watch their actions and assign fitting retribution."}]},{"i":557,"j":570,"loc":"Chapter XXXVIII (5 of 5)","title":"God cannot be bribed; repentance","summary":"The third objection admits God and providence but holds that he can be bought off with offerings by the wicked. Simplicius answers that leaving vice unpunished would harm the sinner, as no honest physician lets a patient eat what made him ill, and would betray the victims, as no general or shepherd betrays his charge. Offerings please God only from the pious or from those who truly seek reform. Phrases about God turning away describe our turning away from him, like sailors who think they pull a rock toward the boat, and repentance means giving up the sin itself. He judges this objection the worst of the three, since being evil is worse than not existing, and closes by calling these beliefs the root of all virtue.","ideas":["Impunity would harm the wicked and betray the injured","God's punishments are medicine for souls","Repentance is our return to God, like hauling a boat to a rock","Holding God corruptible is worse than denying him"],"terms":[{"t":"metanoia (repentance)","d":"Turning back to God, shown truly only by giving up the sin and avoiding even lesser faults."}]},{"i":571,"j":587,"loc":"Chapter XXXIX","title":"How and when to consult oracles","summary":"Epictetus advises approaching divination without desire or aversion, since the event asked about is outside our power and therefore neither good nor bad, and then obeying the god's counsel. Simplicius treats divination as a duty touching both God and ourselves and explains that oracles should be consulted only about matters that reason, art and experience cannot settle. Questions about the soul's immortality should be answered by demonstration, which gives knowledge rather than mere faith, and duties such as defending a friend or country need no oracle. He tells two Delphic stories: Apollo expels a man who left his friend to die among robbers, but welcomes one who killed his friend by accident while trying to save him. He concludes that intention, not outcome, makes an act good or bad.","ideas":["Come to the oracle without hope or fear, then obey it","Consult only on matters no human skill can decide","Demonstration gives knowledge; divine testimony gives belief","Apollo judges intention, not result"],"terms":[{"t":"manteia (divination)","d":"Consulting the gods about uncertain future events, proper only where reason and art cannot decide."},{"t":"prokoptōn (proficient)","d":"One partway along in philosophy, to whom these precepts are chiefly addressed."}]},{"i":588,"j":610,"loc":"Chapters XL–XLIV","title":"Consistent character, silence, laughter and oaths","summary":"Turning to duties toward oneself, Epictetus bids the proficient fix on a character and keep it in public and private; Simplicius cites Socrates' unchanging face. Silence is recommended because speech draws the mind outward, as the Pythagoreans' five-year silence shows; one should speak only of necessary things, briefly, avoid common topics like races and feasts, and above all avoid judging or comparing other people. Where indecent talk cannot be checked, one keeps silent. Laughter should be rare and moderate, since excessive mirth, like excessive grief, upsets the mind. Oaths, which call God as witness, should be avoided except in real necessity, and once sworn must be kept.","ideas":["Keep one consistent character in every setting","Silence keeps the soul turned inward","Talking of others feeds pride and rash judgment","An oath invokes God and must not be taken lightly"],"terms":[{"t":"Pythagorean silence","d":"The five years of silence imposed on new Pythagorean disciples, cited as an extreme model of restraint in speech."}]},{"i":611,"j":627,"loc":"Chapters XLV–XLVI","title":"Feasts, and bodily needs as measure","summary":"Epictetus advises avoiding common feasts; Simplicius contrasts philosophers' banquets, as in the Symposia of Plato, Xenophon and Plutarch, with gluttonous ones, and warns that vice is contagious when one must attend. Chapter XLVI makes the body's needs the rule for food, clothing, housing and servants. The body is the soul's tool, like a carpenter's axe, which needs sharpness, not gilding. Simplicius praises plain diet and native cloth, attacks luxurious houses and crowds of idle footmen who become spies and nuisances, and cites Socrates' single garment, Crates' tub, Epictetus living alone, and Diogenes throwing away his cup.","ideas":["A table with no conversation is a manger","Bad company stains even the pure","Supply the body as a tool, not an ornament","Excess comes from either luxury or vanity"],"terms":[{"t":"chreia (use, need)","d":"The body's real requirement, which sets the limit for all provision made for it."}]},{"i":628,"j":644,"loc":"Chapters XLVII–XLVIII","title":"Chastity, rebuke, and love of praise","summary":"Epictetus urges chastity before marriage, or at least lawful conduct, and forbids harshness toward weaker people. Simplicius argues that sexual desire can be tamed by habit, as fasting, athletes' training and the incest taboo show, and that a man should bring his wife the same purity he expects from her. Rebuke that boasts of one's own virtue provokes resentment and corrupts the rebuker. A gap in the source then cuts the rest of the comment and Chapter XLVIII's text. The surviving part analyses vainglory: it mimics virtue, may restrain the young, but is the soul's inmost garment, leaving inner vice untouched, and only sincere modesty cures it.","ideas":["Appetites can be gently reduced by custom","Boastful reproof hardens the person reproved","Seeking praise empties good acts of virtue","Pretended humility is itself pride"],"terms":[{"t":"philodoxia (love of glory)","d":"Desire for others' praise, the last passion the soul sheds, which makes good acts aim at reputation."}]},{"i":645,"j":661,"loc":"Chapters XLIX–LI","title":"Theatres, recitations and dealing with the great","summary":"Epictetus says to avoid theatres, and if present to want only what happens, keep composed, and not talk much of the show afterwards; Simplicius lists legitimate reasons for going, such as festivals, custom or testing oneself. At public recitations of orators and poets one should be grave but not morose, praising fairly without envy or carping. When dealing with powerful men one should ask how Socrates and Zeno would act. Simplicius advises steering between flattery and rudeness and praising before reproving, like honey on a bitter pill, but warns that a beginner must not mimic such models, citing Antigonus' awe before Zeno.","ideas":["Be indifferent to who wins at the games","Excited talk about shows reveals the mind was captured","Grave but not morose at recitations","Imitate Socrates and Zeno in proportion to one's level"],"terms":[]},{"i":662,"j":677,"loc":"Chapters LII–LV","title":"Visiting great men; conversation and obscenity","summary":"Epictetus advises foreseeing rebuffs before calling on a great man and not complaining afterwards; Simplicius compares the advice with Chapter IX on bathing, noting that this one stresses forethought. He then gives rules for ordinary company: do not tell long stories of one's own deeds, which tire listeners and hint at contempt for others; do not play the buffoon, which cheapens a man. Obscene talk should be rebuked when age, standing and hope of success allow, and otherwise met with a pointed, disapproving silence. Simplicius notes Epictetus' care in matching each response to the situation.","ideas":["Count the cost before courting the powerful","Self-praise is a veiled attack on others","The jester loses respect","Obscenity calls for rebuke or a pointed silence"],"terms":[]},{"i":678,"j":693,"loc":"Chapters LVI–LVII","title":"Resisting pleasure; acting openly","summary":"Epictetus counsels pausing when pleasure appears, comparing its brief moment with lasting regret and the satisfaction of self-control; even innocent pleasures should be kept in check. Simplicius adds that God made bodily pleasures short-lived, that the memory of them is as faint as a dream, and that grief at losing possessions outlasts the joy of having them. Chapter LVII says that what is judged right should be done openly, without fear of others' opinion. Simplicius argues that hiding a right act out of fear of censure prizes a seeming evil over a real good, treats reason's decisions as evils, and hands one's judgment over to common opinion.","ideas":["Weigh the moment of pleasure against long regret","Repeated refusal makes the appetite yield","Do right openly, not in hiding","Fear of censure surrenders reason to opinion"],"terms":[{"t":"hēdonē (pleasure)","d":"Belongs to the senses rather than reason; short-lived, but leaving a sting in the soul."}]},{"i":694,"j":711,"loc":"Chapters LVIII–LX","title":"Fair shares, fitting roles, guarding judgment","summary":"Epictetus compares the proposition 'it is day or night', true taken disjunctively but false conjoined, with taking the biggest share at a feast, which suits one's body but breaks the common right of the guests. Simplicius explains Stoic disjunctive and conditional syllogisms, extends the lesson to all dealings, notes that even robbers keep fair shares, and calls the chapter a lesson in justice against greed. Chapter LIX warns against roles beyond one's ability, since one fails and misses a fitting role, as on a stage where praise goes to performance. Chapter LX compares guarding the mind to watching one's step: passion is like a nail in the foot, false judgment like a twisted ankle.","ideas":["A man at table is part of a conjunction, not a single item","Grasping for more dissolves society","Better a good usher than a bad master","Passion and false judgment are the soul's two injuries"],"terms":[{"t":"hypothetical syllogism","d":"Stoic argument form, either disjunctive (either/or) or conditional (if-then), used here as an image for social obligation."}]},{"i":712,"j":731,"loc":"Chapters LXI–LXII, LXIV","title":"The shoe and the foot; women; injuries","summary":"Epictetus makes the body the measure of possessions, as the foot measures the shoe; once past that limit desire runs on to gilt, purple and jewels without end. Simplicius applies this to getting wealth, as the earlier chapter applied it to using it. Chapter LXII says women courted from fourteen should learn that modesty, not adornment, wins respect; Simplicius blames men for rewarding beauty, and the text then breaks off, with Chapter LXIII lost. Chapter LXIV teaches bearing insult: every man acts on his own opinion, and a false opinion harms only the one who holds it, just as denying a true conditional leaves it true.","ideas":["Needs are bounded; luxury is boundless","Men create the vanity they blame in women","Each acts on his own opinion","The insulter injures himself, not his target"],"terms":[]},{"i":732,"j":747,"loc":"Chapters LXV–LXVIII","title":"Two handles; judging others; hidden philosophy","summary":"Epictetus' image of the two handles teaches taking a brother's wrong by the bearable side, the kinship. Simplicius generalizes from fire's two qualities and runs through riches, poverty, health, sickness, marriage, childlessness, insults, pain and enemies, each with a soft and a hard handle, citing Plutarch on profiting from enemies. Chapter LXVI denies that being richer or more eloquent makes one better, and Simplicius takes it as a rebuke to philosophers' fussy prose. Chapter LXVII forbids judging an act as bad from outward signs without knowing the intention, a matter of justice to a neighbour's reputation. Chapter LXVIII bids one not to profess philosophy but to show it, like Socrates.","ideas":["Every circumstance can be grasped by a bearable handle","Possessions and style are not the man","Moral quality lies in intention, which we rarely see","Show philosophy by example, not by talk"],"terms":[]},{"i":748,"j":764,"loc":"Chapters LXIX–LXXI","title":"Digesting doctrine; secret austerities","summary":"Epictetus tells the learner not to talk philosophy among ordinary people and to bear being thought ignorant, as sheep show their pasture in wool and milk, not by vomiting grass. Simplicius notes that such a person is a proficient, not yet a philosopher, as melting bronze is not a statue; doctrines must first be digested into action. He cites Socrates sending enquirers to Protagoras and Prodicus. Chapter LXX forbids boasting of austerities such as drinking water or embracing statues in the cold; trials should be secret, such as spitting out cold water when parched, and they prepare one for real hardship. Chapter LXXI contrasts the ordinary man, who looks outward for good and evil, with the philosopher, who looks inward.","ideas":["A learner should not teach or display doctrines","Bearing a charge of ignorance shows vanity is mastered","Austerity done for show is wasted","Voluntary hardship trains for forced hardship"],"terms":[{"t":"idiōtēs (layman)","d":"The untrained person who expects good and evil from outside himself."}]},{"i":765,"j":781,"loc":"Chapters LXXII–LXXIII","title":"Marks of the proficient; reading Chrysippus","summary":"Epictetus lists the marks of a proficient: he blames and praises no one, does not boast, blames himself, guards himself like an enemy, as a recovering patient fears relapse. Simplicius sorts people into the layman, the philosopher and the proficient in between, and explains that reason's goods, such as justice, are shared without loss, while bodily goods are divisible and cause strife. Chapter LXXIII mocks pride in expounding Chrysippus: understanding the book makes one a grammarian, not a philosopher. Simplicius adds that an unreformed reader of Chrysippus is more guilty than a reader of Homer, like a sick man who knows the cure and will not take it.","ideas":["Three classes: layman, proficient, philosopher","Goods of reason are shared; bodily goods divide","The proficient watches himself like an enemy","Explaining a moral text without practising it is guilt"],"terms":[{"t":"prokopē (progress)","d":"The middle state between the untaught and the sage, to which the whole Manual is addressed."}]},{"i":782,"j":797,"loc":"Chapters LXXIV–LXXV","title":"Perseverance and the urgency of now","summary":"Epictetus bids the reader hold his rules as binding laws and ignore mockery; Simplicius notes that one swallow does not make a summer and links the advice to the Pythagorean rule not to look back in a temple. Chapter LXXV urges immediate action: the reader has the teachings and a mature age, so delay only cheats himself. Simplicius stresses that in virtue no one stands still, that each neglected day strengthens vice while each kept day builds habit, and that this life is one stage between pre-existence and the state after death. Socrates is offered as a model to live up to even if one cannot equal him.","ideas":["Moral commitments bind like solemn promises","Postponing reform is a debtor's empty promise","Every single act shifts the soul up or down","Live as if meaning to equal Socrates"],"terms":[]},{"i":798,"j":820,"loc":"Chapters LXXVI–LXXIX","title":"The three parts of philosophy; closing sayings","summary":"Epictetus divides philosophy into practice (do not lie), demonstration (why not to lie) and logic (what a demonstration is), and complains that we invert their order. Simplicius explains that logic serves demonstration and both serve practice, the true end. The Manual closes with Cleanthes' prayer to be led by Zeus and Fate, a line of Euripides on yielding to necessity, and Socrates' words from the Crito and Apology that Anytus and Melitus can kill but not harm him. Simplicius reads these as teaching that turning to God and accepting his will crowns all virtue. He says the commentary consoled him in a time of public calamity and ends with a prayer for right use of freedom and clear sight.","ideas":["Logic serves demonstration; both serve right action","We argue well against vices we practise","Willing submission to God's order is the highest virtue","The end returns to the beginning: good lies in our choice"],"terms":[{"t":"Cleanthes' Hymn","d":"Verses asking Zeus and Fate to lead the speaker, who will follow willingly rather than be dragged."}]}]});